WEBVTT - Sol Keeper Project Presentation Captions

00:00.000 --> 00:05.500
When astronauts establish bases on the Moon and Mars, survival isn’t just about courage—it’s an unyielding math problem.

00:05.500 --> 00:10.500
Every watt of electricity, every breath of oxygen, and every gram of water must be accounted for.

00:10.500 --> 00:15.000
Yet space education for kids often stays trapped in posters and abstract facts.

00:15.000 --> 00:19.500
Meet Sol Keeper. We built a single, deterministic space outpost simulation engine and created the Reality Dial.

00:19.500 --> 00:25.000
For younger cadets ages 7 to 12, the station speaks in intuitive status words and icons.

00:25.000 --> 00:29.500
For middle schoolers, it speaks in real units. For high schoolers and teachers, it surfaces the underlying physics equations.

00:29.500 --> 00:35.000
The simulation underneath never changes—only how it’s explained.

00:35.000 --> 00:40.000
Nothing in Sol Keeper is invented silently. Every constant traces back to authenticated NASA standards:

00:40.000 --> 00:45.000
BVAD metabolic rates, OCHMO radiation ceilings, and Ames mass margins.

00:45.000 --> 00:52.000
When a solar storm strikes, it’s driven by NASA DONKI space weather telemetry. When crew members conduct research, they cite NASA OSDR publications.

00:52.000 --> 01:00.000
Real planetary orbital photography from NASA Moon Trek and Mars Trek brings every landing site to life.

01:00.000 --> 01:06.000
In Sol Keeper, no choice is free. Running the Brine Processor recovers 98% of your water, but costs 1.5 crew-hours every single day.

01:06.000 --> 01:14.000
Enriching greenhouse CO₂ speeds up crop growth, but risks lethal hypoxia if untracked.

01:14.000 --> 01:25.000
Every incident response forces genuine trade-offs between crew fatigue, scarce spare parts, and permanent hardware efficiency.

01:25.000 --> 01:33.000
Beyond our five standard planetary missions, students can step into history: improvising the Apollo 13 square-to-round filter adapter,

01:33.000 --> 01:39.000
racing to regolith shelters during the August 1972 solar storm, or containing the 1997 Mir space station fire.

01:39.000 --> 01:45.000
Afterward, students test their grasp with Big Idea concept checks and share reproducible run links with their teachers.

01:45.000 --> 01:52.000
Sol Keeper requires zero logins, zero downloads, and works completely offline as a Progressive Web App in English and Bangla.

01:52.000 --> 02:00.000
Tested across 75 test suites and 4,050 balance seeds, it’s built for real classrooms and real young astronauts. Welcome to deep space survival.

